WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.192
Once the chunker works, we apply it to every source document.

00:00:04.192 --> 00:00:08.202
Each chunk becomes its own row in a new DataFrame.

00:00:08.202 --> 00:00:14.777
We give it a chunk ID and keep the original document ID, title, topic, permission, update date,

00:00:14.777 --> 00:00:16.952
and source. This is a very common RAG data model.

00:00:16.952 --> 00:00:23.407
[tts-break] The vector represents the chunk text, but the metadata travels with that vector.

00:00:23.407 --> 00:00:29.042
When retrieval returns a vector match, we need to know which source document it came from,

00:00:29.042 --> 00:00:38.232
what title to display, whether the user has permission to see it, and whether the source is current.

00:00:38.232 --> 00:00:42.662
Notice that we are not losing the relationship between chunk and document.

00:00:42.662 --> 00:00:49.172
One document may generate several chunks, but every chunk can still point back to the original source.

00:00:49.172 --> 00:00:55.675
That relationship becomes especially useful for citations, access control, and evaluation.
